  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST supply the review prompt that each group's reviewer follows. The prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files. It MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and to return design questions for the user.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has three obligations, and the marked test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_makes_mechanical_changes_and_returns_design_questions]` covers each of them. It reads the prompt through `review_prompt()`, which loads `references/review-prompt.md` from the `habeas-requirements` skill. This also checks that the skill supplies the prompt. The text is whitespace-collapsed with `collapse`, and `assert_text` asserts that the text contains each phrase:\n\n- **Do not edit files.** The test asserts the phrase \"Don't edit files; make your changes in the revised text you return\".\n- **Make mechanical changes in the returned revised text.** The test asserts \"**Mechanical changes:** make them yourself in the revised text, and never phrase them as questions\".\n- **Return design questions for the user.** The test asserts \"**Design questions:** return these; the user decides\".\n\nThe requirement is about the content of a prompt, so phrase-presence assertions are an appropriate check. `assert_text` fails with a message when a phrase is missing, so the assertions do enforce the text. The requirement has no other modes, boundaries or failure cases. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
